WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.589
I often get asked the question about the
WHY in for-profit organizations versus

00:00:04.589 --> 00:00:08.429
not-for-profit organizations. So before I
answer the question, let me just take a

00:00:08.429 --> 00:00:12.599
little aside. I hate the term
"not-for-profit"' I hate the idea that an

00:00:12.599 --> 00:00:16.199
organization and a group of people are
defining their purpose based on what

00:00:16.199 --> 00:00:19.650
they're not. We have to stand for
something, not against things.

00:00:19.650 --> 00:00:24.269
The reality is "not-for-profit" is a tax
delineation and that's why we use the term.

00:00:24.269 --> 00:00:28.560
And not-for-profits absolutely have an

00:00:28.560 --> 00:00:31.920
idea of profit except it's defined differently. 
In a for-profit organization,

00:00:31.920 --> 00:00:36.300
profit is defined in money.
In a not-for-profit organization, profit is

00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:39.750
defined by whatever you're trying to
accomplish. So for example, if your

00:00:39.750 --> 00:00:43.890
objective is to rescue kittens out of trees,
then you better be profitable.

00:00:43.890 --> 00:00:48.030
You better be risking more kittens out of trees
this month then you were last month.

00:00:48.030 --> 00:00:52.590
Let's call them "for impact" organizations.
So we have for-profit organizations

00:00:52.590 --> 00:00:56.610
and we have for impact organizations.
Ok, now back to the question.

00:00:56.610 --> 00:01:00.899
The WHY is different and, sometimes, 
how do we compare the WHYs of for-profit

00:01:00.899 --> 00:01:04.890
versus for impact organizations -  
here's generally what I've seen.

00:01:04.890 --> 00:01:09.630
For impact organizations are pretty good
at articulating their WHY.

00:01:09.630 --> 00:01:12.810
Where they're weak is in their HOWs, 
the structures in which they use to

00:01:12.810 --> 00:01:13.987
advance their cause.

00:01:13.987 --> 00:01:17.340
I mean definitions, as we've just discussed,
is one of them.

00:01:17.340 --> 00:01:21.990
For profit organizations tend to be really good at HOW
and pretty weak in WHY.

00:01:21.990 --> 00:01:25.259
In other words, they're really good at process,
they're really good at operations,

00:01:25.259 --> 00:01:29.070
but the sense of higher purpose and higher cause
seems to be lacking.

00:01:29.070 --> 00:01:33.299
So what both organizations can learn to do is
learn from each other.

00:01:33.299 --> 00:01:37.979
In other words, for impact organizations can learn to
bolster operations from for-profit,

00:01:37.979 --> 00:01:41.130
and for-profit could learn a little bit
about working towards a higher cause

00:01:41.130 --> 00:01:44.520
from for impact organizations.
They should look the same.

00:01:44.520 --> 00:01:49.170
Remember, it's the definition, it's just the
metric that is different.

00:01:49.170 --> 00:01:52.290
But the organization should feel the same to work there.
In other words, we should feel

00:01:52.290 --> 00:01:55.170
like we're part of something bigger than ourselves
and it should be a well-oiled machine,

00:01:55.170 --> 00:01:59.310
and we should have good metrics
to help us feel like we're

00:01:59.310 --> 00:02:01.525
moving the ball down the playing field.

00:02:01.525 --> 00:02:03.570
Back to the for impact organizations.

00:02:03.570 --> 00:02:05.770
Because there were poor metrics and
because they do not define themselves as

00:02:05.770 --> 00:02:09.759
for impact, unfortunately the metric becomes
too much about money and raising money

00:02:09.759 --> 00:02:13.690
and less about understanding 
how far we're moving the ball down the field

00:02:13.690 --> 00:02:18.750
with whatever we're trying to accomplish.
So there we go.

